body.p:
Claim 31 recites a method for non-invasive determination of a hemodynamic parameter of a subject mechanically ventilated via a breathing apparatus based on a point in time, thb, of aheartbeat of the subject and an arrival point in  SVG
{EMBED HERE}
0.140.827 at which a blood pressure pulsecaused by the heartbeat reaches lungs of the subject, comprising "determining the point in time, thb, of the heartbeat of the subject" and "obtaining a respiratory pressure and/or a respiratory flow via one of a pressure sensor and a flow sensor of the breathing apparatus" along with "determining, via a controller of the breathing apparatus, tarr_pulm from a change in the measured respiratory pressure and/or the respiratory flow resulting from a change in lung volume caused by the arrival of the blood pressure pulse to the lungs" and "determining, via the controller, a pulmonary pulse transit time, PTTpulm, for the blood pressure pulse based on thb and tarr pulm" and "determining, via the controller, the hemodynamic parameter based on PTTpulm."

It is respectfully submitted that Luxon does not show or suggest "determining, via acontroller of the breathing  SVG
{EMBED HERE}
0.1430.97 pum from a change in the measured respiratorypressure and/or the respiratory flow resulting from a change in lung volume caused by the arrival of the blood pressure pulse to the lungs" along with "determining, via the controller, apulmonary pulse transit time, PTTpuim, for the blood pressure pulse based on thb and  SVG
{EMBED HERE}
0.1430.203 pum" and"determining, via the controller, the hemodynamic parameter based on PTTpuim," as recited in claim 31. Rather, Luxon uses a magnitude of cardiogenic oscillations to monitor cardiac function and determine hemodynamic parameters. Luxon does not show or suggest that these cardiac oscillations are in any way related to a time of arrival of a blood pressure pulse to the lungs of the subject so that a pulmonary pulse transit time may be determined therefrom, and then subsequently used to determine the hemodynamic parameter.

Accordingly, it is submitted that Luxon does not show or suggest "determining, via acontroller of the breathing  SVG
{EMBED HERE}
0.1430.97 pum from a change in the measured respiratorypressure and/or the respiratory flow resulting from a change in lung volume caused by the arrival of the blood pressure pulse to the lungs" along with "determining, via the controller, apulmonary pulse transit time, PTTpuim, for the blood pressure pulse based on thb and  SVG
{EMBED HERE}
0.1430.203 pum" and"determining, via the controller, the hemodynamic parameter based on PTTpuim," as recited in claim 31. Thus, it is respectfully submitted that claim 31 is not anticipated by Luxon and that the rejection of this claim should be withdrawn. Because claims 32-39 and 41 depend from and include all of the limitations of claim 31, it is respectfully submitted that these claims are also allowable.
